losely, I used two different headers - one with `Authorization: Bearer ...` and another with `X-API-Key: ...`. The first one returned data while the second returned 401, which means the Bearer token format is what the API expects for GET requests. For POST, I need to figure out if the key format changes or if there's something else going on with how the endpoint validates credentials.\n\nLet me test if the /send endpoint might accept query string authentication instead - I could pass the API key as a URL parameter and see if that bypasses the authentication issue.\n\nActually, I'm realizing the /send endpoint might have different auth requirements than /history, or the POST request body itself is being validated differently. Let me try encoding the API key in the request body itself rather than the header - some APIs expect credentials passed directly in the JSON payload for certain operations","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\n\n"},{"type":"thinking","thinking":".\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call-e1b03a3a-75d6-41aa-8599-7db2856a3aa3","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"curl -s -X POST \"https://echo-agent-platform.zhouyating.workers.dev/send\" \\\n  -H \"X-API-Key: echo-agent-2026-secure-key\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"threadId\":\"arbitration\",\"content\":\"test\",\"apiKey\":\"echo-agent-2026-secure-key\"}' && echo \"\""}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"nvidia","model":"minimaxai/minimax-m2.7","usage":{"input":35111,"output":649,"cacheRead":112,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":35872,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1778955489485,"responseId":"chatcmpl-389f7a7a-34e0-434c-b8f9-e3efd6afcf86"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call-e1b03a3a-75d6-41aa-8599-7db2856a3aa3","toolName":"exec","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"id\":\"msg_1778955502480_ljo2j0\",\"content\":\"test\",\"threa